+### Combining coverage from sharded test runs
+
+When you shard your test runs (for example, across multiple CI jobs), each shard produces its own coverage data. To apply a coverage threshold to the combined result, first collect the per-shard coverage output, then combine it before checking the threshold.
+
+Run each shard with coverage enabled and the `raw` report format so that the underlying coverage data files are preserved:
+
+```toml title="pants.toml"
+[coverage-py]
+report = ["raw"]
+```
+
+```bash
+# In each shard, run a subset of the tests with coverage.
+❯ pants test --use-coverage <shard-targets>
+```
+
+After all shards have finished, gather the raw coverage data files from each shard's output directory (see `[coverage-py].output_dir`) into a single location, then combine them with Coverage.py's `combine` command:
+
+```bash
+❯ python -m coverage combine path/to/shard1 path/to/shard2 ...
+```
+
+Finally, apply the threshold to the combined data with `coverage report --fail-under`:
+
+```bash
+❯ python -m coverage report --fail-under=70
+```
+
+This lets you enforce a single coverage threshold over the union of all shards, rather than per-shard thresholds that could each pass while the overall coverage is too low.
+
 You may use a Coverage config file, e.g. `.coveragerc` or `pyproject.toml`. Pants will autodiscover the config file for you, and you can also set `[coverage-py].config` in your `pants.toml` to point to a non-standard location.
 
 :::note You must include `relative_files = true` in the `[run]` section for Pants to work:
